    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d74d2450-a86e-5747-8d99-f726055086ba",
      ":question/problem": "Let $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$ and points the same direction.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u} &= \\frac{\\mathbf{b}}{|\\mathbf{b}|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\mathbf{b}| &= \\sqrt{(-3)^2+(4)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25} \\\\\n&= 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $\\mathbf{b}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u} &= \\frac{\\mathbf{b}}{|\\mathbf{b}|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
